//! #7437, re-pinned by #7966: a NUL byte glued to a number token was part of that token for jq
//! 1.7.1 and 1.8.2, whose number parser reads the token as a C string and so stops at the NUL:
//! `[1`, NUL, `x,2]` and a newline is `[1,2]` in both releases. Under ADR-0018's #7963 amendment E2
//! there is no NUL-ended number: a NUL outside a string is `Invalid character` at its own line and
//! column, the token before it checked first, as jq master (unreleased; commit fbee8f4,
//! jqlang/jq#3606) reads it. So every row below differs from both releases, which print the number.
//!
//! The expected messages were generated by running `succinctly jq` over each row and checked, row
//! by row, against a driver built over jq master's `jv_parse.c` (`jv_parser_next` over the row's
//! bytes): the same message, line and column for all 491 rows, and as many values before it. The
//! rows' inputs are those #7437 captured from jq 1.7.1, so each row's jq answer is its number read
//! up to the NUL (`[1,2]`, `[12,2]`, `[1E+5,2]`, `[null,2]` for `nan`), or the refusal of a token
//! jq refused then (`-`, `1e`, `n`, a NUL in a string).

use anyhow::Result;

